---
// Smart Formatting (2.5.0 and 2.5.1 What's New): spoken numbers, dates, versions,
// hosts, addresses and slash commands written the way you would type them, on
// the Mac, before any AI polish. Every example is a row in
// help/numbers-dates-and-times.md or help/spoken-punctuation-and-emoji.md,
// which own the behavior; the long-form twin is the formatting blog post.
import FeatureShell from '../../layouts/FeatureShell.astro';
import '../../styles/features/everyday.css';
import FeatureHero from '../../components/features/FeatureHero.astro';
import FeatureFilm from '../../components/features/FeatureFilm.astro';
import ImportReassurance from '../../components/features/ImportReassurance.astro';
import FeatureFaq from '../../components/features/FeatureFaq.astro';
import DownloadClosing from '../../components/DownloadClosing.astro';
const everyday = [
  ['six thousand two hundred thirty nine dollars', '$6,239'],
  ['july eleventh twenty twenty six', 'July 11, 2026'],
  ['six fifty p m', '6:50 PM'],
  ['eight two four six one nine six one seven five', '824-619-6175'],
  ['john dot smith at gmail dot com', 'john.smith@gmail.com'],
];
const technical = [
  ['version two point five point zero', 'version 2.5.0'],
  ['localhost colon three thousand', 'localhost:3000'],
  ['one nine two dot one six eight dot one dot one', '192.168.1.1'],
  ['twenty twenty six dash nine dash twenty six', '2026-09-26'],
  ['S dash one', 'S-1'],
  ['slash clear', '/clear'],
];
const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Can I dictate version numbers and IP addresses on a Mac?',
    a: 'Yes. In EnviousWispr, a free dictation app for Mac, saying "version two point five point zero" gives version 2.5.0, and "one nine two dot one six eight dot one dot one" gives 192.168.1.1. Hosts and ports work too: "localhost colon three thousand" becomes localhost:3000.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I dictate a slash command like /clear?',
    a: 'Say "slash clear" and EnviousWispr writes /clear, even with Convert spoken punctuation switched off. "pros slash cons" becomes pros/cons, and an ordinary phrase like "slash the budget" stays as words. Some verb uses, like "slash prices", can still become a symbol.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need AI polish for spoken formatting?',
    a: 'No. Formatting runs on your Mac before any AI polish, automatically, and there is nothing to set up. Spoken number words are converted in English; in other languages the speech engine usually writes digits itself. If polish is off or hits an error, the formatted text is what gets pasted. A polish model that runs may still reword it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens with a phrase that could mean two things?',
    a: 'EnviousWispr leaves it as you said it rather than guessing. "Meet at one twenty" is a time and "paid one twenty" is money, so an ambiguous phrase stays in words for you or the polish step to decide.',
  },
];
---

<FeatureShell
  path="/features/smart-formatting/"
  title="Smart Formatting: dictate numbers, dates and code on Mac"
  description="Say localhost colon three thousand and get localhost:3000. EnviousWispr writes numbers, dates, versions, links and slash commands the way you type them."
  ogImage="/images/og/smart-formatting.jpg"
  ogImageAlt="EnviousWispr Smart Formatting: say it the way you talk, get it the way you type"
>
  <FeatureHero
    kind="smart-formatting"
    label="Smart Formatting"
    title={'Say it the way you talk.<br><span class="accent-ink">Get it the way you type.</span>'}
    copy="EnviousWispr is a free dictation app for Mac. When you say a price, a date, a phone number, a version or a web address, it writes it the way you would have typed it. This happens on your Mac, before any AI step, whenever you dictate in English, and addresses, links and codes are formatted in eight more languages."
  >
    <FeatureFilm kind="formatting" />
  </FeatureHero>
  <section class="benefit wrap reverse">
    <div class="benefit-copy">
      <span class="eyebrow">Everyday writing</span>
      <h2>Money, dates and phone numbers,<br /><span class="accent-ink">written out for you.</span></h2>
      <p>Dictation usually hands you the words, spelled out, and leaves you to turn them into digits. When you dictate in English, EnviousWispr does that part. Numbers from ten upwards become digits, while smaller ones stay as words, the way most style guides write them.</p>
      <p>A full US street address comes out with its digits and commas, and a spoken email address comes out ready to send.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="benefit-visual">
      <div class="say-get" aria-label="Everyday examples of spoken formatting">
        {
          everyday.map(([said, got]) => (
            <div class="say-get-row">
              <span class="said">“{said}”</span>
              <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              <span class="got">{got}</span>
            </div>
          ))
        }
        <span class="say-get-note">What you say, and what EnviousWispr writes.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="benefit wrap">
    <div class="benefit-copy">
      <span class="eyebrow">For developers</span>
      <h2>Versions, hosts and commands<br /><span class="accent-ink">come out whole.</span></h2>
      <p>Talking through a bug report or a pull request means saying things like version numbers, ports and IP addresses. EnviousWispr writes them as one piece: 2.5.0, localhost:3000, 192.168.1.1. Dates said with dashes become 2026-09-26, and a code like "S dash one" becomes S-1.</p>
      <p>Spoken slash commands work with Convert spoken punctuation switched off. "slash clear" becomes /clear, which helps when you dictate into a terminal or a chat tool that takes commands.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="benefit-visual">
      <div class="say-get" aria-label="Technical examples of spoken formatting">
        {
          technical.map(([said, got]) => (
            <div class="say-get-row">
              <span class="said">“{said}”</span>
              <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              <span class="got">{got}</span>
            </div>
          ))
        }
        <span class="say-get-note">What you say, and what EnviousWispr writes.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="benefit wrap reverse">
    <div class="benefit-copy">
      <span class="eyebrow">Careful by design</span>
      <h2>When in doubt,<br /><span class="accent-ink">your words stay as you said them.</span></h2>
      <p>Formatting runs before AI polish, so if polish is off or fails, the formatted text is still what gets pasted. When a phrase could mean two things, such as "one twenty", EnviousWispr leaves it as words rather than guessing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="benefit-visual">
      <ImportReassurance
        wrap={false}
        items={[
          { title: 'On your Mac', copy: 'Formatting is a local step. It sends nothing anywhere.' },
          { title: 'Nothing to set up', copy: 'It is switched on when you install the app.' },
          { title: 'In more languages', copy: 'Addresses, links and codes said in French, Spanish, Polish, Dutch, German, Russian, Portuguese or Italian come out written too.' },
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="matching-story plain wrap">
    <h2>Every rule,<br /><span class="accent-ink">with examples.</span></h2>
    <p>Street addresses, web addresses, small numbers, other languages and the phrases EnviousWispr leaves alone are all covered in the help centre. Spoken punctuation such as "comma" and "new line" is a separate setting, off by default.</p>
    <a class="story-link" href="/help/numbers-dates-and-times/">Read the full list of what gets formatted <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
  </section>
  <FeatureFaq items={faq} />
  <DownloadClosing title="Dictation that writes the way you type." />
</FeatureShell>
